{
  "image_name": "2b7833e8182341b815367665bec4dfe6.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a proposed link between periodontal infection and joint inflammation in rheumatoid arthritis. It centers on *P. gingivalis* in the periodontium, whose enzyme PPAD drives citrullination of host or bacterial proteins. In a genetically susceptible person, indicated by the HLA‑DRB1 shared epitope, these altered proteins are taken up by antigen-presenting cells, leading to T-cell activation, then B-cell and plasma-cell responses, and ultimately production of ACPAs.\n\nThese ACPAs are then shown binding citrullinated host proteins to form immune complexes. The same citrullination process appears to occur more broadly, generating targets for these antibodies. In the joint, the ACPA-containing immune complexes engage leukocytes through Fc receptors and also activate complement. This produces anaphylatoxins that signal through complement receptors such as C3aR or C5aR, further stimulating inflammatory cells. The end result is release of inflammatory mediators within synovial tissue.\n\nOverall, the image illustrates a mechanistic pathway in which oral infection and protein citrullination may initiate an autoimmune response that spreads to the joints, where immune complexes and complement amplify inflammation.",
